WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
Three to four extra rare vision-loss cases per 10,000 showed up in an observational glp-1 study. This is peptides weekly, episode five, education and evidence, never-dosing advice. Rutgers researchers followed adults in the United States who had type 2 diabetes and had just begun treatment. ScienceDailyNUA.News both described the paper in annals of internal medicine. The comparison is observational. People starting a glp-1 receptor agonist had a higher chance of a schematic optic neuropathy.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:33.440
which happens when blood flow to the optic nerve drops.

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:36.860
Chintan Dave at Rutgers said the condition matters

00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:38.600
because vision can fail suddenly,

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:40.980
even though the added risk is small.

00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:44.320
Semaglutide, sold as ozempic and wagovi,

00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:47.740
and tear-zepatide, sold as mongaro and zepbound,

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:50.900
are approved drugs used for blood sugar and body weight.

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:54.680
The class can also lower the risk of some heart and kidney problems.

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:57.500
On estimates cited by UA.News,

00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:02.660
more than 80% of these optic nerve cases are the non-artoritic anterior form.

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:06.660
They showed up more often in men and in people from 50 to 65.

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:11.440
The authors said other illnesses could explain the pattern, so the link is not

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:15.540
proof the drugs damaged the nerve. They also said a sudden change in sight

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:20.480
should not wait on an eye exam. On October 8th, this show was on nails and hair with

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:24.860
these medicines. Today's paper is a different measured risk in medical records,

00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:31.890
not a repeat of that side-effect list. Researchers at UC Santa Cruz are pointing at a peptide called P3.

00:01:32.630 --> 00:01:37.470
Science daily says it had been treated as harmless for years. This is not a study in people.

00:01:38.070 --> 00:01:44.870
The report says P3 can form harmful looking clumps even faster than amyloid beta, and that it may injure brain cells.

00:01:45.530 --> 00:01:50.810
Amyloid beta has been the main Alzheimer's target for decades, and many of those drugs disappointed.

00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:56.810
This write-up puts P3 next to that peptide as another possible contributor without offering

00:01:56.810 --> 00:01:57.790
a treatment result.

00:01:58.799 --> 00:02:02.299
Verdiva has stopped a mid-stage study of an oral GLP1.

00:02:02.939 --> 00:02:05.639
BioSpace says the company pointed to market evolution.

00:02:06.239 --> 00:02:08.899
That is a decision about an investigational program.

00:02:09.419 --> 00:02:12.619
It is not a published trial result, and it is not an approval.

00:02:13.179 --> 00:02:17.259
The report does not include efficacy or safety figures from the study that ended.

00:02:17.259 --> 00:02:23.439
Medical Daily says the FDA is weighing wider access to peptides, while adverse event reports

00:02:23.439 --> 00:02:26.919
and two intubations in Las Vegas sharpen the safety questions.

00:02:27.439 --> 00:02:29.999
The piece is tied to BPC157.

00:02:30.399 --> 00:02:33.959
That is news about harm reports in policy, not a clinical trial.

00:02:34.539 --> 00:02:39.219
Products sold outside ordinary drug controls can also differ in purity and contamination.

00:02:40.481 --> 00:02:46.401
Buchanan Ingersol and Rooney describes a temporary FDA enforcement reprieve on peptide compounding,

00:02:46.401 --> 00:02:48.401
and what it could mean for pharmacies.

00:02:48.861 --> 00:02:50.621
A policy pause is not evidence

00:02:50.621 --> 00:02:53.101
that a compounded peptide works or is safe.

00:02:53.701 --> 00:02:55.941
A compounded preparation is not the same thing

00:02:55.941 --> 00:02:57.701
as a fully reviewed approved medicine.

00:02:58.461 --> 00:03:00.041
A physician at MU health care

00:03:00.041 --> 00:03:02.821
is warning people away from unregulated peptides.

00:03:03.621 --> 00:03:05.281
KOMU eight reported the warning.

00:03:05.881 --> 00:03:08.381
That is clinical commentary, not a new experiment.

00:03:09.121 --> 00:03:11.301
Peptides sold without regulatory controls

00:03:11.301 --> 00:03:13.121
can differ in purity and contamination

00:03:13.121 --> 00:03:15.661
from anything studied under a trial protocol.

00:03:16.573 --> 00:03:24.133
BPC-157, the compound in this week's spotlight, is 15 amino acids long, a fragment of a body

00:03:24.133 --> 00:03:27.093
protection peptide isolated from human gastric juice.

00:03:27.733 --> 00:03:32.053
The groups that study it treated as a healing signal that also touches nitric oxide.

00:03:32.673 --> 00:03:34.733
The gas vessels used to manage blood flow.

00:03:35.253 --> 00:03:41.673
In a 1997 rat paper, stomach tissue homogenates made nitric oxide when the peptide was added,

00:03:41.673 --> 00:03:48.813
at a level comparable to the amino acid L-arginine. L-name, a blocker of the enzyme that makes nitric oxide,

00:03:49.333 --> 00:03:55.073
stopped the L-arginine effect and did not stop the peptide effect. Putting the peptide and L-arginine

00:03:55.073 --> 00:04:01.473
together blunted nitric oxide synthesis in that dish. In the same rat work, the peptide reduced alcohol

00:04:01.473 --> 00:04:06.933
injury to the stomach lining and changed blood pressure responses to L-name and L-arginine

00:04:06.933 --> 00:04:10.133
in a pattern that was not a copy of L. Arginine alone.

00:04:10.633 --> 00:04:17.133
A 2014 review reads this as the peptide joining the nitric oxide response after a vessel is damaged.

00:04:17.673 --> 00:04:23.213
Depending on the injury model, the authors describe less clotting or less bleeding and fewer platelets.

00:04:23.733 --> 00:04:28.813
That review also says the peptide turns on the genes EGR1 and NAB2.

00:04:28.813 --> 00:04:36.773
A 2018 review sets it beside EGF, FGF, and VGF, and says its own angiogenic effect appears

00:04:36.773 --> 00:04:41.853
in the healing models those authors surveyed. The tendon evidence is rat tissue on a bench,

00:04:42.213 --> 00:04:48.433
not patients. Explans grew out faster. An MTT assay showed no direct boost in fibroblast

00:04:48.433 --> 00:04:54.733
proliferation. Cells given the peptide survived hydrogen peroxide stress better. They migrated more

00:04:54.733 --> 00:04:58.613
as the amount rose, spread faster on dishes, and built F-Actin.

00:04:59.393 --> 00:05:04.313
Phosphorylation of FAK and Paxilin rose with the amount of peptide, while the total amount

00:05:04.313 --> 00:05:05.893
of those proteins stayed the same.

00:05:06.513 --> 00:05:11.433
The author's Thai Outgrowth, Stress Survival, and Migration to that FAK Paxilin Path.

00:05:11.853 --> 00:05:18.113
A 2026 critical review, with searches through the 24th of June that year, looks at rodent

00:05:18.113 --> 00:05:19.773
ischemia-reperfusion models.

00:05:19.773 --> 00:05:25.053
angles. Across those papers, the peptide was associated with less oxidative injury, shifts

00:05:25.053 --> 00:05:31.053
in nitric oxide vessel responses, and lower inflammatory and apoptotic markers. The same

00:05:31.053 --> 00:05:37.773
review describes changes in VEGF R2, AKT, and E&amp;O signaling. Reported settings include

00:05:37.773 --> 00:05:43.833
leg muscle ischemia reperfusion, distant organs after a limb injury, the hippocampus, intestine

00:05:43.833 --> 00:05:46.253
and colon and a hepatic pringle maneuver.

00:05:46.813 --> 00:05:48.633
The reviewers did not pool the numbers.

00:05:49.253 --> 00:05:52.173
Organs, roots, timing, and outcomes were too mixed.

00:05:52.713 --> 00:05:53.953
Follow-up was often short.

00:05:54.513 --> 00:05:58.693
Many designs used a single dose, and risk of bias was incompletely described.

00:05:59.273 --> 00:06:04.273
They also say blocking a major vessel is not the same experiment as classic organ ischemia

00:06:04.273 --> 00:06:05.033
reperfusion.

00:06:05.493 --> 00:06:11.633
A 2018 review claims the peptide, given alone, was consistently effective in acute and chronic

00:06:11.633 --> 00:06:17.713
injury models of esophagus, stomach, duodenum, and lower gut, by injection into the abdomen,

00:06:18.033 --> 00:06:23.393
by mouth, or locally. That review also says the same experimental regimens improve tendon,

00:06:23.793 --> 00:06:28.533
ligament, and bone healing in the studies surveyed. Those are animal models summarized by

00:06:28.533 --> 00:06:34.833
reviewers, not outcomes measured in patients. A 2013 review calls it proven safe in inflammatory

00:06:34.833 --> 00:06:43.893
bowel disease trials coded PL-10, PL-D-116, and PL-14736, and in wound healing

00:06:43.893 --> 00:06:48.153
and says no toxicity was reported, and a lethal dose was never reached.

00:06:48.613 --> 00:06:52.573
That abstract does not say how many people were enrolled or what the trials measured

00:06:52.573 --> 00:06:53.853
beyond that safety claim.

00:06:54.433 --> 00:06:59.993
The same review argues the peptide can counter several NSAID injuries in gut, liver, and brain,

00:06:59.993 --> 00:07:05.013
plane, plus effects on bleeding and clots, from the experimental work the authors assemble.

00:07:05.693 --> 00:07:11.333
The 2014 review says turning nitric oxide modulation into better clinical performance remains

00:07:11.333 --> 00:07:12.153
to be determined.

00:07:12.813 --> 00:07:18.193
A separate 2026 material's paper built a kytosan hydrogel carrying the peptide.

00:07:18.913 --> 00:07:23.753
Infrared spectroscopy showed hydrogen bonds between the peptide and the kytosan backbone,

00:07:24.313 --> 00:07:25.513
and the gel was porous.

00:07:25.513 --> 00:07:31.093
Water vapor transmission was about 2,270 grams per square meter per day.

00:07:31.493 --> 00:07:40.673
With a spread of 35, encapsulation was 98.9%, plus or minus 0.8, and 81.2%, plus or

00:07:40.673 --> 00:07:43.873
minus 2.9, came out within 24 hours.

00:07:44.413 --> 00:07:50.433
Inhibition reached 45.9% against E. coli and 65% against staff aureus.

00:07:50.433 --> 00:07:56.673
Himalosus stayed under 5%, which the authors read as acceptable hemocompatibility in that

00:07:56.673 --> 00:07:59.393
lab test, not as a human safety result.

00:07:59.933 --> 00:08:04.193
None of these abstracts stayed in approval, an indication, or a regulator's decision.

00:08:04.833 --> 00:08:07.993
Name trial codes are not the same thing as a licensed medicine.

00:08:08.713 --> 00:08:11.733
Older reviews describe a wide pre-clinical safety margin.

00:08:12.213 --> 00:08:16.993
That is not a finished human safety record, and it says nothing about purity when a peptide

00:08:16.993 --> 00:08:18.773
is sold outside a controlled study.

00:08:18.773 --> 00:08:24.606
One thing worth knowing is that a record's link between starting a GLP1 medicine, and

00:08:24.606 --> 00:08:28.646
a rare optic nerve injury is a signal, not proof the drug caused it.

00:08:29.106 --> 00:08:33.266
Another is that a rare association can still matter when millions of people take a drug,

00:08:33.686 --> 00:08:37.426
which is why the authors called the finding clinically important while still calling

00:08:37.426 --> 00:08:38.686
the absolute risk low.

00:08:39.346 --> 00:08:44.246
A third is that ending a mid-stage oral GLP1 study is a development choice about an

00:08:44.246 --> 00:08:48.986
investigational peptide, not a verdict that the class failed or succeeded in people.

00:08:49.506 --> 00:08:50.586
That's peptides weekly.

00:08:51.206 --> 00:08:53.546
This show is education, not medical advice.

00:08:54.106 --> 00:08:56.786
Talk to your own clinician before acting on anything you heard.

